// Module 9 quiz - Deep Learning for AI Startups
$CATEGORY: $course$/Module 9

::M9 Q1:: Why can accuracy be misleading? {
~It cannot be computed
~It ignores the model
~It only works for images
=On imbalanced data, a model can score high by always predicting the common class#If 90% of items are normal, a model that always says normal is 90% accurate and finds nothing.
}

::M9 Q2:: What does low agreement between two labelers suggest? {
=Labeling guidelines are unclear#When careful people disagree, the rules for deciding are missing or vague. Fix the guide before the model.
~The model is perfect
~The dataset is too clean
~The GPU is faulty
}

::M9 Q3:: Why keep a fixed test set that is never trained on? {
~To make training faster
~To increase dataset size
=For an honest, comparable measure of performance over time#If the test set never changes and is never trained on, scores from different weeks and different models can be compared fairly.
~To avoid writing code
}

::M9 Q4:: What is the purpose of failure analysis? {
~Hide mistakes
=Find the biggest categories of error and prioritize fixes#Counting errors by cause shows where a fix pays off most.
~Reduce the dataset
~Choose a font
}

::M9 Q5:: Which metric matters most when missing a positive case is very costly? {
~Training time
~Parameter count
~Batch size
=Recall#Recall is the share of real positives that the model finds, so it falls when positives are missed.
}
